---
# Which files this rule applies to, as a YAML list — Claude Code's native
# shape. Cursor's comma-separated `globs` is derived from it at render time,
# never authored here.
#
# Omit `paths` entirely to make the rule always-on: loaded on every turn, for
# every agent, whether or not it's relevant. There is no `alwaysApply` field,
# because "scoped *and* always-on" is a contradictory state that shouldn't be
# expressible. Always-on rules are charged against the ceiling `rule-budget.py`
# enforces, so reach for `paths` first.
paths:
  - "**/*.component.ts"
  - "**/*.directive.ts"

# One line, shown to the agent when it decides whether to load this rule. Write
# it so that decision is obvious from the description alone.
description: Angular component and reactivity conventions

# The practice note or notes this rule was distilled from, by slug — a slug is
# the note's filename without the extension, the same identifier a [[wikilink]]
# uses. One or several: a rule is routinely distilled from more than one note,
# and naming one of seven leaves the other six reading as never promoted.
#
#   source: prefer-signal-apis
#   source: [prefer-signal-apis, next-server-components-by-default]
#
# Never rendered — it reaches no `.mdc` or `.claude/rules/*.md` output. It
# exists so `check-lineage.py` can cross-check the capture side (automated,
# writes notes) against the promotion side (human, writes rules): which
# enforced notes still have no rule, and which rules cite a note that has been
# deleted or demoted. A rule with no `source:` is not a smaller problem than a
# wrong one — with nothing declared, neither question can be answered at all.
source: [prefer-signal-apis]
---

Imperative bullets, one convention each. This is the body an agent actually
reads, so write what to do rather than why it was decided — the reasoning
belongs in the practice note this was distilled from.

- Use signal APIs (`input()`, `output()`, `computed()`), not decorators, in new
  code.
- Default every component to `OnPush`.
